WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:04.437
<i> NARRATOR: In a faraway place,</i>

00:00:04.437 --> 00:00:07.807
<i> there's a magical time</i>

00:00:07.807 --> 00:00:10.676
<i> when the sun never sets.</i>

00:00:10.676 --> 00:00:13.580
(roar)

00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:17.283
<i> Now, way up north,</i>

00:00:17.283 --> 00:00:19.352
<i> take a giant leap...</i>

00:00:19.352 --> 00:00:24.657
♪ ♪

00:00:24.657 --> 00:00:29.595 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> ...and swoop through
 Scandinavia's Arctic Circle</i>

00:00:29.595 --> 00:00:33.166
<i> for a season of sunshiny days.</i>

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:36.535
<i> Norway.</i>

00:00:36.535 --> 00:00:38.237
<i> Sweden.</i>

00:00:38.237 --> 00:00:40.706
<i> Finland.</i>

00:00:40.706 --> 00:00:44.444
<i> But be quick.</i>

00:00:44.444 --> 00:00:49.248 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> The light, bright magic
 doesn't last long...</i>

00:00:49.248 --> 00:00:50.115
(hiss)

00:00:50.115 --> 00:00:52.618
<i> ...at the top of the world.</i>

00:00:52.618 --> 00:00:54.654
♪ ♪

00:00:54.654 --> 00:01:00.760
♪ ♪

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:07.633
♪ ♪

00:01:07.633 --> 00:01:10.669
<i> Svalbard Archipelago.</i>

00:01:10.669 --> 00:01:14.139 align:start size:78% position:22%
<i> This is inside
 the Arctic Circle</i>

00:01:14.139 --> 00:01:17.410
<i> and as far north
 as Norway gets.</i>

00:01:21.981 --> 00:01:24.951
<i> It's the kind of place
 most people think of</i>

00:01:24.951 --> 00:01:26.986
<i> when they hear "Arctic."</i>

00:01:26.986 --> 00:01:32.091
♪ ♪

00:01:32.091 --> 00:01:35.795
<i> Svalbard is a cluster
 of extraordinary islands</i>

00:01:35.795 --> 00:01:38.998
<i> and icebergs full of life.</i>

00:01:38.998 --> 00:01:43.636
♪ ♪

00:01:43.636 --> 00:01:46.372
<i> Female walrus have
 migrated north,</i>

00:01:46.372 --> 00:01:51.644
<i> preferring to chase the thick
 ice that they love so much.</i>

00:01:51.644 --> 00:01:53.446
<i> But the males stay here</i>

00:01:53.446 --> 00:01:57.817 align:start position:0%
<i> and look for places to hang out
 for summer.</i>

00:01:57.817 --> 00:01:59.385
<i> Kicking back in Svalbard</i>

00:01:59.385 --> 00:02:03.356 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> has its disadvantages
 for the males.</i>

00:02:03.356 --> 00:02:06.025
<i> They're exposed to predators.</i>

00:02:09.929 --> 00:02:12.298
<i> Polar bears.</i>

00:02:12.298 --> 00:02:13.966
(roar)

00:02:13.966 --> 00:02:18.003
♪ ♪

00:02:18.003 --> 00:02:24.977
♪ ♪

00:02:24.977 --> 00:02:29.281 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> This one has already
 killed a walrus.</i>

00:02:29.281 --> 00:02:34.754
<i> He'll eat as much as 20% of
 his body weight in a sitting.</i>

00:02:34.754 --> 00:02:40.894 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> Then, once it's done, time
 again to look for fresh meat.</i>

00:02:44.797 --> 00:02:46.899
<i> Polar bears hold the title</i>

00:02:46.899 --> 00:02:50.102 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
<i> as the world's largest
 land predators.</i>

00:02:50.102 --> 00:02:54.807 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
<i> But in reality, they track
 their food on water--</i>

00:02:54.807 --> 00:02:56.976
<i> frozen water.</i>

00:02:59.111 --> 00:03:03.616 align:start position:0%
<i> They use the ice as a platform
 from which to hunt.</i>

00:03:05.184 --> 00:03:09.021 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> Jumping from ice floe
 to ice floe.</i>

00:03:09.021 --> 00:03:12.091 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> Now that most of the day
 has sunshine,</i>

00:03:12.091 --> 00:03:14.527
<i> their ice is getting thin.</i>

00:03:22.768 --> 00:03:29.341
(groaning)

00:03:29.341 --> 00:03:31.043
<i> They can shake off a quick dip</i>

00:03:31.043 --> 00:03:36.115 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> in the freezing water
 without fuss.</i>

00:03:36.115 --> 00:03:39.484
<i> So walrus and seals
 are right to worry</i>

00:03:39.484 --> 00:03:42.822
<i> when there's a bear around.</i>

00:03:42.822 --> 00:03:46.292
<i> They have just one consolation.</i>

00:03:46.292 --> 00:03:50.496 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> Polar bears don't rest
 in winter like other bears.</i>

00:03:50.496 --> 00:03:55.468 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> But in summer, they may fast
 for months at a time.</i>

00:03:57.569 --> 00:03:59.404
<i> This is how summer plays out</i>

00:03:59.404 --> 00:04:04.744
<i> in this northernmost
 outpost of Norway.</i>

00:04:04.744 --> 00:04:06.379
<i> But travel south,</i>

00:04:06.379 --> 00:04:10.116 align:start position:0%
<i> and there's more to the Arctic
 than you know.</i>

00:04:14.954 --> 00:04:18.290 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> Svalbard is an outpost
 of Norway</i>

00:04:18.290 --> 00:04:22.228
<i> in the country's far north.</i>

00:04:22.228 --> 00:04:26.131 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> But the Arctic Circle
 is much more,</i>

00:04:26.131 --> 00:04:29.568 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> an invisible line that rings
 the top of the globe</i>

00:04:29.568 --> 00:04:34.540 align:start size:78% position:22%
<i> at 66 degrees,
 33 minutes north.</i>

00:04:34.540 --> 00:04:40.747
<i> The Scandinavia section covers
 about 150,000 square miles.</i>

00:04:43.716 --> 00:04:47.386 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> And it's topped by
 the North Cape Monument,</i>

00:04:47.386 --> 00:04:51.691
<i> the northernmost point
 of mainland Europe.</i>

00:04:53.192 --> 00:04:57.996
<i> This is the end of the earth.</i>

00:04:57.996 --> 00:05:04.870
<i> The North Cape Monument sits
 atop a 1,000-foot-high plateau</i>

00:05:04.870 --> 00:05:06.338
<i> and looks out to where</i>

00:05:06.338 --> 00:05:10.142
<i> the Atlantic meets
 the Arctic Ocean.</i>

00:05:10.142 --> 00:05:13.178
♪ ♪

00:05:13.178 --> 00:05:19.352
♪ ♪

00:05:19.352 --> 00:05:21.620
<i> Everywhere in the Arctic Circle</i>

00:05:21.620 --> 00:05:26.626 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> just had at least a month
 of perpetual darkness.</i>

00:05:26.626 --> 00:05:29.829
<i> But in February
 the light returns.</i>

00:05:32.498 --> 00:05:35.501 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> And now you can see
 the northernmost points</i>

00:05:35.501 --> 00:05:38.037
<i> of Scandinavia's mainland.</i>

00:05:41.340 --> 00:05:44.477 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> At the very bottom
 of the Arctic Circle,</i>

00:05:44.477 --> 00:05:48.981
<i> spring has already cleared
 the snow from the peaks.</i>

00:05:51.783 --> 00:05:56.889 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> Here in Norway,
 the Arctic becomes...</i>

00:05:56.889 --> 00:05:59.592
<i> a hiker's paradise.</i>

00:05:59.592 --> 00:06:03.662
♪ ♪

00:06:03.662 --> 00:06:08.134 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> This is
 the Lofoten Archipelago.</i>

00:06:10.136 --> 00:06:13.105
<i> Several hundred remote islands.</i>

00:06:13.105 --> 00:06:17.142
♪ ♪

00:06:17.142 --> 00:06:21.280
♪ ♪

00:06:22.781 --> 00:06:26.552
<i> Cliffs to the north are
 an annual summer retreat</i>

00:06:26.552 --> 00:06:30.389
<i> for one of the strangest
 creatures in the Arctic.</i>

00:06:30.389 --> 00:06:35.627
(squawking)

00:06:35.627 --> 00:06:39.899 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> Around 160,000 puffins
 vacation here</i>

00:06:39.899 --> 00:06:43.069 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> for a couple of months
 every year.</i>

00:06:50.209 --> 00:06:53.112 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> Summer is the puffins'
 breeding season,</i>

00:06:53.112 --> 00:06:55.548 align:start size:81% position:19%
<i> when they get
 to know each other.</i>

00:06:58.083 --> 00:07:00.719
<i> They build nests
 in rock crevices</i>

00:07:00.719 --> 00:07:03.523
<i> and in holes among the stones.</i>

00:07:11.797 --> 00:07:14.900
<i> And of course, fill up on fish.</i>

00:07:14.900 --> 00:07:16.935
♪ ♪

00:07:16.935 --> 00:07:23.709
♪ ♪

00:07:23.709 --> 00:07:27.380
<i> They are capable flyers,
 and even better swimmers.</i>

00:07:28.914 --> 00:07:31.584 align:start size:81% position:19%
<i> And puffins are one
 of the few birds</i>

00:07:31.584 --> 00:07:36.321
<i> that can hold many fish
 in their beaks at once.</i>

00:07:36.321 --> 00:07:40.092
<i> Their raspy tongues give them
 just the right kind of grip</i>

00:07:40.092 --> 00:07:43.729 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> so they can bring back
 fresh fish for their young.</i>

00:07:45.230 --> 00:07:47.666
<i> Puffins usually mate for life,</i>

00:07:47.666 --> 00:07:50.769 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> and both parents will share
 the responsibility</i>

00:07:50.769 --> 00:07:53.005
<i> of caring for their young.</i>

00:07:53.005 --> 00:07:54.706
<i> Similar to penguins,</i>

00:07:54.706 --> 00:07:58.310
<i> the flightless birds
 of the other pole.</i>

00:07:58.310 --> 00:08:02.515 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> But despite their similar
 colors, they are not related.</i>

00:08:04.083 --> 00:08:08.154 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> The puffins' bright-colored
 bills match their feet.</i>

00:08:08.154 --> 00:08:11.023
<i> But only during
 the mating season.</i>

00:08:14.493 --> 00:08:18.731
<i> Before summer is done, they'll
 shed that colorful outer layer</i>

00:08:18.731 --> 00:08:23.336
<i> and head back out to sea with
 smaller, dull-colored beaks.</i>

00:08:25.404 --> 00:08:28.407 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> While they don't mind
 chatting up a storm now...</i>

00:08:28.407 --> 00:08:30.710
(squawking)

00:08:30.710 --> 00:08:34.714 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> ...once at sea they remain
 perfectly silent.</i>

00:08:40.886 --> 00:08:44.122 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> Meanwhile, some of the fish
 they like to hunt</i>

00:08:44.122 --> 00:08:46.892
<i> have their own summer plans.</i>

00:08:46.892 --> 00:08:52.798
♪ ♪

00:08:52.798 --> 00:08:54.700
<i> Millions head inland</i>

00:08:54.700 --> 00:08:58.737
<i> along the great waterways
 of Scandinavia's Arctic.</i>

00:08:58.737 --> 00:09:02.908
♪ ♪

00:09:02.908 --> 00:09:06.879 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> They return from the ocean
 and charge upstream.</i>

00:09:10.749 --> 00:09:13.619
<i> Among them, grayling.</i>

00:09:16.054 --> 00:09:20.592 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> These are relics
 of the last Ice Age,</i>

00:09:20.592 --> 00:09:25.565 align:start position:0%
<i> and they perform an annual
 ritual of frenzied egg-laying.</i>

00:09:27.132 --> 00:09:32.071
<i> Here, two males flank a female.</i>

00:09:32.071 --> 00:09:36.208
<i> And both try to fertilize
 her eggs at the same time.</i>

00:09:36.208 --> 00:09:38.243
♪ ♪

00:09:38.243 --> 00:09:44.283
♪ ♪

00:09:44.283 --> 00:09:50.489
♪ ♪

00:09:50.489 --> 00:09:57.462
♪ ♪

00:09:57.462 --> 00:09:59.565
♪ ♪

00:10:04.436 --> 00:10:09.275
<i> The volume of life in these
 pristine waters during summer</i>

00:10:09.275 --> 00:10:13.312 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> attracts fishermen
 from all over Scandinavia.</i>

00:10:13.312 --> 00:10:19.885
♪ ♪

00:10:19.885 --> 00:10:23.521 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> The Tana River is
 one of the few places</i>

00:10:23.521 --> 00:10:26.092
<i> where they can still use nets.</i>

00:10:28.294 --> 00:10:31.563 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> Sometimes following
 the traditional methods</i>

00:10:31.563 --> 00:10:36.935
<i> of the Sami people who've lived
 here for at least 2,000 years.</i>

00:10:36.935 --> 00:10:40.973
♪ ♪

00:10:40.973 --> 00:10:46.679
♪ ♪

00:10:46.679 --> 00:10:47.947
<i> Around the world,</i>

00:10:47.947 --> 00:10:51.349 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> summer signals a time
 for water sports.</i>

00:10:51.349 --> 00:10:56.522 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> And the Arctic Circle
 is no different.</i>

00:10:56.522 --> 00:11:01.426
<i> The potential thrills outweigh
 the chills for Arctic surfers.</i>

00:11:01.426 --> 00:11:03.462
♪ ♪

00:11:03.462 --> 00:11:09.134
♪ ♪

00:11:09.134 --> 00:11:13.005 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> The waves roll in
 all the way from Greenland.</i>

00:11:14.673 --> 00:11:18.210 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> It's the most reliable surf
 in Northern Europe.</i>

00:11:18.210 --> 00:11:21.947
<i> And that makes it a magnet
 for die-hard board riders</i>

00:11:21.947 --> 00:11:25.584 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> like local Tommy Olsen
 and his son.</i>

00:11:29.554 --> 00:11:31.289
TOMMY OLSEN: You have
these point breaks,

00:11:31.289 --> 00:11:33.859
which in surfing terms
is like really good.

00:11:33.859 --> 00:11:37.262 align:start size:94% position:6%
The wave breaks all the time
on the same spot.

00:11:37.262 --> 00:11:38.864
Normally have, like, one or two,

00:11:38.864 --> 00:11:40.699 align:start size:88% position:13%
but here you have,
like, three point breaks

00:11:40.699 --> 00:11:42.868
and also a beach break.

00:11:44.403 --> 00:11:47.272
<i> NARRATOR: The Gulf Stream
 keeps this Arctic water</i>

00:11:47.272 --> 00:11:51.877 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> between 46 and 58 degrees
 year round.</i>

00:11:51.877 --> 00:11:56.481
<i> But the air temperature can
 easily dip below freezing.</i>

00:11:56.481 --> 00:11:58.517
♪ ♪

00:11:58.517 --> 00:12:05.224
♪ ♪

00:12:05.224 --> 00:12:10.796
<i> For Tommy, the chill and
 the challenge is worth it.</i>

00:12:10.796 --> 00:12:12.331
TOMMY: It is quite unique.

00:12:12.331 --> 00:12:15.200
You know, the waves are
really world-class waves.

00:12:15.200 --> 00:12:22.174
♪ ♪

00:12:22.174 --> 00:12:29.014
♪ ♪

00:12:29.014 --> 00:12:30.949 align:start size:97% position:3%
But the thing is the backdrop,
the scenery,

00:12:30.949 --> 00:12:34.453
is it's really different
from other surf spots.

00:12:37.022 --> 00:12:39.457
<i> NARRATOR: People have come
 from all over the world</i>

00:12:39.457 --> 00:12:42.594
<i> to surf here since the '60s,</i>

00:12:42.594 --> 00:12:47.031
<i> and Lofoten has the world's
 northernmost surf schools.</i>

00:12:47.031 --> 00:12:49.368
<i> There are two of them in town.</i>

00:12:51.536 --> 00:12:55.340
<i> The surfers ride as long as
 there are waves and light.</i>

00:12:55.340 --> 00:12:59.945
♪ ♪

00:12:59.945 --> 00:13:05.450 align:start position:0%
<i> And up here, the sun in summer
 can shine 24 hours a day.</i>

00:13:05.450 --> 00:13:10.756
♪ ♪

00:13:10.756 --> 00:13:14.526 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> This endless summer makes up
 for that time of year</i>

00:13:14.526 --> 00:13:18.264
<i> when the lights
 once again go off.</i>

00:13:20.699 --> 00:13:25.537 align:start position:0%
<i> Ice and cold take over the land
 as early as August.</i>

00:13:25.537 --> 00:13:27.139
(creaking)

00:13:27.139 --> 00:13:34.113
♪ ♪

00:13:34.113 --> 00:13:35.581
(creaking)

00:13:37.649 --> 00:13:39.384
<i> The days get shorter;</i>

00:13:39.384 --> 00:13:42.721 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> 15 minutes less light
 every day.</i>

00:13:45.824 --> 00:13:49.094
<i> And that makes what
 Arctic surfers do next</i>

00:13:49.094 --> 00:13:51.096
<i> even more amazing.</i>

00:13:55.533 --> 00:13:58.903
<i> NARRATOR: Arctic surfers still
 find their way to the waves</i>

00:13:58.903 --> 00:14:02.407
<i> even as summer wanes.</i>

00:14:02.407 --> 00:14:04.509
<i> Now at night they can be guided</i>

00:14:04.509 --> 00:14:09.414
<i> by the beguiling green
 of the northern lights.</i>

00:14:09.414 --> 00:14:14.352 align:start position:0%
<i> It's the kind of magic you can
 only find near the poles.</i>

00:14:14.352 --> 00:14:18.323
♪ ♪

00:14:18.323 --> 00:14:23.862
♪ ♪

00:14:23.862 --> 00:14:26.698 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> On the ground
 the Arctic Circle is marked</i>

00:14:26.698 --> 00:14:30.802
<i> by little more than
 an occasional road,</i>

00:14:30.802 --> 00:14:33.605 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> and this visitor's center
 that stays open</i>

00:14:33.605 --> 00:14:37.743
<i> until the beginning of October.</i>

00:14:37.743 --> 00:14:41.913
<i> When the roads and paths
 become clogged with snow,</i>

00:14:41.913 --> 00:14:45.083 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> and the trees become
 ice sculptures,</i>

00:14:45.083 --> 00:14:48.720 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> it's time to release
 the hounds.</i>

00:14:48.720 --> 00:14:50.756
(barking)

00:14:50.756 --> 00:14:57.529
(barking)

00:14:57.529 --> 00:15:03.635
♪ ♪

00:15:03.635 --> 00:15:09.741
♪ ♪

00:15:09.741 --> 00:15:15.313 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> Dog sledding is popular
 across Scandinavia,</i>

00:15:15.313 --> 00:15:20.185 align:start position:0%
<i> and races are
 the Arctic Circle's Grand Prix.</i>

00:15:20.185 --> 00:15:21.185
(growl)

00:15:21.185 --> 00:15:24.890
<i> Perhaps even more grueling.</i>

00:15:24.890 --> 00:15:29.127 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> Some of the events cover
 more than 700 miles</i>

00:15:29.127 --> 00:15:32.798
<i> and last for five or six days.</i>

00:15:34.098 --> 00:15:35.867
<i> Training for such marathons</i>

00:15:35.867 --> 00:15:40.104
<i> begins as soon as
 the snow sets in,</i>

00:15:40.104 --> 00:15:42.741
<i> even if it's still
 technically summer.</i>

00:15:42.741 --> 00:15:44.776
♪ ♪

00:15:44.776 --> 00:15:51.183
♪ ♪

00:15:51.183 --> 00:15:55.854 align:start position:0%
<i> Eight dogs to a sled,
 and they run as smooth as silk.</i>

00:15:55.854 --> 00:16:00.592
♪ ♪

00:16:00.592 --> 00:16:02.894 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> This mode of transport
 has been around</i>

00:16:02.894 --> 00:16:05.698
<i> for at least 1,000 years.</i>

00:16:09.168 --> 00:16:12.337 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> Roald Amundsen,
 the Norwegian explorer</i>

00:16:12.337 --> 00:16:15.941 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> who became the first person
 to reach the South Pole,</i>

00:16:15.941 --> 00:16:18.010
<i> achieved his epic journey</i>

00:16:18.010 --> 00:16:22.247 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> thanks to such hard-working
 canine companions.</i>

00:16:22.247 --> 00:16:28.620
♪ ♪

00:16:28.620 --> 00:16:35.327
♪ ♪

00:16:35.327 --> 00:16:37.329
♪ ♪

00:16:37.329 --> 00:16:43.335
(howling)

00:16:43.335 --> 00:16:47.405
♪ ♪

00:16:47.405 --> 00:16:53.845
♪ ♪

00:16:53.845 --> 00:16:58.984
<i> When it's all done, the reward
 is a release from the harness.</i>

00:16:58.984 --> 00:17:05.490
♪ ♪

00:17:05.490 --> 00:17:12.464
♪ ♪

00:17:12.464 --> 00:17:19.404
♪ ♪

00:17:19.404 --> 00:17:22.207
<i> The only mush now is dinner.</i>

00:17:22.207 --> 00:17:28.246
♪ ♪

00:17:28.246 --> 00:17:35.086
♪ ♪

00:17:35.086 --> 00:17:38.457 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> The sled drivers are
 a diverse group.</i>

00:17:41.526 --> 00:17:45.597 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> But the dogs must be built
 for Arctic extremes.</i>

00:17:45.597 --> 00:17:50.035
♪ ♪

00:17:50.035 --> 00:17:53.171 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> So much so, that these breeds
 happily live</i>

00:17:53.171 --> 00:17:55.807 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> in their outdoor kennels
 year round,</i>

00:17:55.807 --> 00:17:58.109
<i> no matter the weather.</i>

00:17:58.109 --> 00:18:04.215
♪ ♪

00:18:04.215 --> 00:18:09.121
♪ ♪

00:18:10.689 --> 00:18:13.759 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> The wild animals
 in the Arctic Circle</i>

00:18:13.759 --> 00:18:16.728 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> don't have the luxury
 of a kennel.</i>

00:18:19.598 --> 00:18:23.335 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> And in this world of white,
 they stand out.</i>

00:18:23.335 --> 00:18:27.906
♪ ♪

00:18:27.906 --> 00:18:30.642 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> An orderly line of reindeer
 heads north</i>

00:18:30.642 --> 00:18:32.210
<i> at the end of summer.</i>

00:18:32.210 --> 00:18:34.245
♪ ♪

00:18:34.245 --> 00:18:40.985
♪ ♪

00:18:40.985 --> 00:18:44.656
<i> As they roam, they look for
 patches of lichen or moss</i>

00:18:44.656 --> 00:18:47.693 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> to dig up from beneath
 the Arctic snow.</i>

00:18:50.028 --> 00:18:52.064
(snow crunching)

00:18:52.064 --> 00:18:58.536
(snow crunching)

00:18:58.536 --> 00:19:01.273 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> The sun may look like
 it's setting.</i>

00:19:01.273 --> 00:19:05.977 align:start position:0%
<i> But up here in early autumn,
 this is what passes for midday.</i>

00:19:05.977 --> 00:19:09.948
♪ ♪

00:19:09.948 --> 00:19:13.885 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> In about a month,
 the sun won't rise at all.</i>

00:19:13.885 --> 00:19:15.720
<i> And this whole wonderland</i>

00:19:15.720 --> 00:19:19.825 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> will plunge into
 a month of darkness.</i>

00:19:19.825 --> 00:19:23.829
<i> Before that happens,
 there's work to do.</i>

00:19:32.404 --> 00:19:36.775
(engine roaring)

00:19:38.477 --> 00:19:41.212 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> NARRATOR: The Sami people
 of northern Norway</i>

00:19:41.212 --> 00:19:45.750
<i> have herded reindeer here
 for at least 1,000 years.</i>

00:19:45.750 --> 00:19:47.786
♪ ♪

00:19:47.786 --> 00:19:53.825
♪ ♪

00:19:53.825 --> 00:20:00.165
♪ ♪

00:20:00.165 --> 00:20:04.336 align:start position:0%
MAN: It's not so much of a job,
it's more like a lifestyle.

00:20:06.037 --> 00:20:10.142 align:start size:97% position:3%
Herding the reindeers,
you get like a deep connection

00:20:10.142 --> 00:20:13.578 align:start size:91% position:9%
to the nature and
a respect for the nature.

00:20:13.578 --> 00:20:19.617
♪ ♪

00:20:19.617 --> 00:20:25.657
♪ ♪

00:20:25.657 --> 00:20:31.696
♪ ♪

00:20:31.696 --> 00:20:38.303
♪ ♪

00:20:38.303 --> 00:20:41.305 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> NARRATOR: Now with the help
 of machines and dogs,</i>

00:20:41.305 --> 00:20:45.143 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> they give the animals a nudge
 in the right direction,</i>

00:20:45.143 --> 00:20:49.715
<i> through temperatures
 of minus 40 degrees.</i>

00:20:51.349 --> 00:20:55.153
MAN: Somehow the reindeer
actually doesn't even mind

00:20:55.153 --> 00:20:57.589
the cold weather.

00:21:00.758 --> 00:21:04.496 align:start size:88% position:13%
The reindeer has
a special kind of hair.

00:21:04.496 --> 00:21:07.398 align:start size:84% position:16%
It's like a pipe
that has holes in it,

00:21:07.398 --> 00:21:09.234
and it insulates much better

00:21:09.234 --> 00:21:12.871 align:start size:88% position:13%
than other types of hair
on other animals.

00:21:12.871 --> 00:21:19.310
♪ ♪

00:21:19.310 --> 00:21:26.284
♪ ♪

00:21:26.284 --> 00:21:33.124
♪ ♪

00:21:33.124 --> 00:21:34.192
<i> NARRATOR: All summer</i>

00:21:34.192 --> 00:21:38.062 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> the reindeer have been
 free to graze.</i>

00:21:38.062 --> 00:21:42.067
<i> Now the Sami round them up
 for a twice-annual check-up.</i>

00:21:45.236 --> 00:21:47.672
<i> They've covered 20 miles today</i>

00:21:47.672 --> 00:21:51.443 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> to get this sea of antlers
 into a corral.</i>

00:21:53.144 --> 00:21:55.813
<i> And now they're nearly there.</i>

00:21:55.813 --> 00:22:01.920
♪ ♪

00:22:01.920 --> 00:22:07.959
♪ ♪

00:22:07.959 --> 00:22:14.199
♪ ♪

00:22:14.199 --> 00:22:18.136
MAN: When we start moving the
reindeer herd to the corrals,

00:22:18.136 --> 00:22:23.241
a lot of family members and
friends come and help us.

00:22:24.809 --> 00:22:26.544 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> NARRATOR:
 It looks like midnight,</i>

00:22:26.544 --> 00:22:29.948
<i> but it's only around
 5:00 in the afternoon.</i>

00:22:32.517 --> 00:22:34.686
<i> As the reindeer
 run in a circle,</i>

00:22:34.686 --> 00:22:36.854
<i> the herders size them up</i>

00:22:36.854 --> 00:22:39.791 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> and separate them
 into different groups.</i>

00:22:41.559 --> 00:22:44.129
<i> Some will get veterinary care.</i>

00:22:44.129 --> 00:22:47.899
<i> Some will get ear tags
 or collars for tracking.</i>

00:22:47.899 --> 00:22:52.871 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> Others will be sectioned off
 for market.</i>

00:22:52.871 --> 00:22:57.008 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> Reindeer skin, horns and bone
 are all important products</i>

00:22:57.008 --> 00:22:58.643
<i> for the Sami people,</i>

00:22:58.643 --> 00:23:02.246 align:start position:0%
<i> and they've traditionally
 depended on them for survival.</i>

00:23:02.246 --> 00:23:08.887
♪ ♪

00:23:08.887 --> 00:23:10.688
<i> And the story goes,</i>

00:23:10.688 --> 00:23:14.292 align:start position:0%
<i> there's another traditional use
 for reindeer.</i>

00:23:14.292 --> 00:23:18.963
♪ ♪

00:23:18.963 --> 00:23:24.002
<i> Reindeer sleds in this part
 of Finland lead to one place.</i>

00:23:25.603 --> 00:23:30.074 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> A tiny hamlet
 famous for one resident.</i>

00:23:30.074 --> 00:23:34.078
♪ ♪

00:23:34.078 --> 00:23:36.781
<i> This is Santa Village,</i>

00:23:36.781 --> 00:23:42.553
<i> with everything Christmas
 inside, including Santa.</i>

00:23:42.553 --> 00:23:47.091 align:start position:0%
<i> It's a tourist not-so-hot spot,
 open all year round,</i>

00:23:47.091 --> 00:23:50.796 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> for those who just
 can't wait for December.</i>

00:23:55.834 --> 00:23:59.037 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> Finding a warm place to stay
 after summer</i>

00:23:59.037 --> 00:24:02.407
<i> can be a challenge.</i>

00:24:02.407 --> 00:24:05.510 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> But since 2008,
 there's been the option</i>

00:24:05.510 --> 00:24:10.748
<i> of the now world famous
 30-room-large snow hotel.</i>

00:24:10.748 --> 00:24:12.784
♪ ♪

00:24:12.784 --> 00:24:18.622
♪ ♪

00:24:18.622 --> 00:24:20.959
<i> Starting in early September,</i>

00:24:20.959 --> 00:24:23.627
<i> artists rebuild
 the accommodations</i>

00:24:23.627 --> 00:24:26.330
<i> by packing snow into blocks.</i>

00:24:26.330 --> 00:24:33.270
♪ ♪

00:24:33.270 --> 00:24:37.742 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> They have supersized the idea
 of a traditional igloo.</i>

00:24:41.913 --> 00:24:45.484
<i> The cocktails at this bar
 are all served chilled.</i>

00:24:48.987 --> 00:24:52.623
<i> Dining area.</i>

00:24:52.623 --> 00:24:56.194
<i> Bedrooms.</i>

00:24:56.194 --> 00:24:58.963
<i> Even a chapel.</i>

00:24:58.963 --> 00:25:01.933
<i> All sheltered from
 the outer elements</i>

00:25:01.933 --> 00:25:05.170 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> and kept at a relatively
 toasty temperature</i>

00:25:05.170 --> 00:25:09.407
<i> just a few degrees
 below freezing.</i>

00:25:09.407 --> 00:25:13.845 align:start position:0%
<i> These are the creature comforts
 of the Arctic Circle.</i>

00:25:16.113 --> 00:25:21.920
<i> But outside, it's more
 about the creatures.</i>

00:25:21.920 --> 00:25:24.055 align:start size:81% position:19%
<i> They have their own
 ways of coping</i>

00:25:24.055 --> 00:25:26.957
<i> with the chill of autumn.</i>

00:25:26.957 --> 00:25:30.294
<i> And some of them are
 downright deadly.</i>

00:25:30.294 --> 00:25:31.429
(hiss)

00:25:43.675 --> 00:25:45.843
<i> NARRATOR: Up in
 the Arctic Circle,</i>

00:25:45.843 --> 00:25:49.247 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> lynx seem to welcome
 the early snow.</i>

00:25:49.247 --> 00:25:53.151
<i> And they are well built for it.</i>

00:25:53.151 --> 00:25:55.854 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> Their long legs
 and wide webbed feet</i>

00:25:55.854 --> 00:25:59.423 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> allow them to move quickly
 across the powder.</i>

00:25:59.423 --> 00:26:05.329
♪ ♪

00:26:05.329 --> 00:26:07.898
<i> And it takes more
 than a light snow</i>

00:26:07.898 --> 00:26:10.902 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> to chill them through
 that thick fur.</i>

00:26:10.902 --> 00:26:13.938
♪ ♪

00:26:13.938 --> 00:26:20.845
♪ ♪

00:26:20.845 --> 00:26:23.481 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> By autumn, the spring kittens
 are big enough</i>

00:26:23.481 --> 00:26:25.416
<i> to keep up with mom.</i>

00:26:25.416 --> 00:26:27.451
♪ ♪

00:26:27.451 --> 00:26:33.758
♪ ♪

00:26:33.758 --> 00:26:38.229 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> But make no mistake about
 who's still in charge.</i>

00:26:38.229 --> 00:26:40.131
(hiss)

00:26:44.902 --> 00:26:48.706 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> Especially when Mom's found
 a scrap of squirrel.</i>

00:26:48.706 --> 00:26:53.411
♪ ♪

00:26:53.411 --> 00:26:57.248 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> They've spent their summer
 being nurtured by her.</i>

00:26:57.248 --> 00:26:59.083
<i> But no more.</i>

00:26:59.083 --> 00:27:01.118
♪ ♪

00:27:01.118 --> 00:27:06.424
♪ ♪

00:27:06.424 --> 00:27:08.893
(hiss)

00:27:08.893 --> 00:27:14.365 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> That's lynx for
 "Wait your turn, Junior."</i>

00:27:14.365 --> 00:27:17.701 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> One day these twins
 will be just as assertive</i>

00:27:17.701 --> 00:27:19.404
<i> with their own young.</i>

00:27:19.404 --> 00:27:23.641 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> Until then, they can practice
 fighting with each other.</i>

00:27:23.641 --> 00:27:28.746
♪ ♪

00:27:28.746 --> 00:27:33.284
♪ ♪

00:27:34.886 --> 00:27:37.789
<i> These kittens are fully weaned,</i>

00:27:37.789 --> 00:27:42.226 align:start position:0%
<i> but may stay with their mother
 for another year.</i>

00:27:42.226 --> 00:27:44.762 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> When they finally
 head out without her,</i>

00:27:44.762 --> 00:27:47.498
<i> they may stick close
 for a few months,</i>

00:27:47.498 --> 00:27:52.203
<i> working together across
 the Arctic landscape,</i>

00:27:52.203 --> 00:27:54.672
<i> and even up the trees.</i>

00:27:54.672 --> 00:27:56.707
♪ ♪

00:27:56.707 --> 00:28:03.748
♪ ♪

00:28:03.748 --> 00:28:10.488
♪ ♪

00:28:10.488 --> 00:28:13.958 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> Eventually, though,
 they'll separate entirely</i>

00:28:13.958 --> 00:28:18.262 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> and lead the solitary life
 of a lone predator,</i>

00:28:18.262 --> 00:28:24.201
<i> searching for food
 in the wild north.</i>

00:28:24.201 --> 00:28:29.073 align:start size:81% position:19%
<i> If they become
 very good hunters,</i>

00:28:29.073 --> 00:28:32.610
<i> there are plenty of
 menu options out here.</i>

00:28:32.610 --> 00:28:37.381
♪ ♪

00:28:37.381 --> 00:28:40.552
<i> Reindeer shelter
 among the trees.</i>

00:28:42.987 --> 00:28:46.424
<i> Summer was easy pickings.</i>

00:28:46.424 --> 00:28:51.062
<i> Now they must search hard for
 new places to rest or graze.</i>

00:28:51.062 --> 00:28:56.634
♪ ♪

00:28:56.634 --> 00:29:01.138 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> When they catch a sniff
 of danger, they have a plan.</i>

00:29:01.138 --> 00:29:05.610
♪ ♪

00:29:05.610 --> 00:29:11.182 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> They use a nearby frozen-over
 river like a freeway.</i>

00:29:11.182 --> 00:29:14.786 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> They are able to move fast
 through the terrain.</i>

00:29:14.786 --> 00:29:16.354
<i> And just as well.</i>

00:29:16.354 --> 00:29:18.389
<i> They run for a reason.</i>

00:29:18.389 --> 00:29:20.424
(wolf howls)

00:29:20.424 --> 00:29:24.763 align:start position:0%
<i> Not far behind,
 a small pack of Arctic wolves.</i>

00:29:29.000 --> 00:29:33.705
<i> Wolves are the second largest
 predator in Western Europe.</i>

00:29:35.606 --> 00:29:38.175
<i> Only bears are bigger.</i>

00:29:38.175 --> 00:29:40.211
♪ ♪

00:29:40.211 --> 00:29:45.449
♪ ♪

00:29:45.449 --> 00:29:48.052
<i> Packs can be 30-strong.</i>

00:29:48.052 --> 00:29:49.920
<i> Three is small.</i>

00:29:49.920 --> 00:29:53.724
<i> But that's more than enough
 to take down a straggler.</i>

00:29:53.724 --> 00:29:58.229
<i> The young and the weak
 are their specialty.</i>

00:29:58.229 --> 00:30:03.100 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> Wolves can easily cover
 12 miles a day.</i>

00:30:03.100 --> 00:30:05.837
<i> And the reindeer have
 made it easier for them,</i>

00:30:05.837 --> 00:30:10.274
<i> creating trails
 through the snow.</i>

00:30:10.274 --> 00:30:12.944 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> As they go,
 they mark their territory</i>

00:30:12.944 --> 00:30:16.481
<i> with urine and sometimes feces.</i>

00:30:16.481 --> 00:30:20.184 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> For wolves, this is a form
 of communication:</i>

00:30:20.184 --> 00:30:22.987
<i> "Interlopers beware."</i>

00:30:25.422 --> 00:30:28.559
<i> Today the reindeer
 have the upper hand.</i>

00:30:28.559 --> 00:30:32.163 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> And the pace of the chase
 is not worth it.</i>

00:30:32.163 --> 00:30:36.133 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> Summer was good
 to this wolf pack trio.</i>

00:30:36.133 --> 00:30:38.703
<i> No need to push it just yet.</i>

00:30:41.339 --> 00:30:43.808
<i> The wolves take a break.</i>

00:30:43.808 --> 00:30:48.412
(whining)

00:30:48.412 --> 00:30:54.685
♪ ♪

00:30:54.685 --> 00:30:57.421
<i> They are fearsome
 Arctic predators.</i>

00:30:57.421 --> 00:30:59.557
<i> And even the giants up here</i>

00:30:59.557 --> 00:31:02.994
<i> must try to stay
 out of their way.</i>

00:31:08.967 --> 00:31:11.635 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> NARRATOR: This moose mother
 and her year-old calf</i>

00:31:11.635 --> 00:31:16.440 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> search for food in
 Norway's Arctic Circle.</i>

00:31:16.440 --> 00:31:18.476
♪ ♪

00:31:18.476 --> 00:31:24.916
♪ ♪

00:31:24.916 --> 00:31:27.218 align:start size:81% position:19%
<i> Their cloven hooves
 spread widely</i>

00:31:27.218 --> 00:31:30.821 align:start size:81% position:19%
<i> to support them
 over the soft snow.</i>

00:31:30.821 --> 00:31:36.193 align:start position:0%
<i> Those long legs can carry them
 through rough terrain.</i>

00:31:36.193 --> 00:31:40.632
<i> Yet they can move
 as quietly as a cat.</i>

00:31:45.903 --> 00:31:49.574 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> It's an extraordinary
 achievement for their size,</i>

00:31:49.574 --> 00:31:53.911 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> but important in helping them
 to avoid predators.</i>

00:31:53.911 --> 00:31:57.548 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> Summer is over and
 the pasture is buried.</i>

00:31:57.548 --> 00:32:02.220
<i> But there's plenty of light
 and still a lot to dine on.</i>

00:32:04.488 --> 00:32:07.892
<i> They live on lichen
 that grows on trees.</i>

00:32:07.892 --> 00:32:13.297
♪ ♪

00:32:13.297 --> 00:32:16.701
<i> If they can, they'll also
 stretch their powerful necks</i>

00:32:16.701 --> 00:32:19.737
<i> upwards to grab pine needles.</i>

00:32:19.737 --> 00:32:22.139
<i> But it's the nibbling
 of the nether regions</i>

00:32:22.139 --> 00:32:24.609
<i> that got them their name.</i>

00:32:26.643 --> 00:32:29.780 align:start size:81% position:19%
<i> "Moose" is derived
 from "moosh,"</i>

00:32:29.780 --> 00:32:31.382
<i> a Native American word</i>

00:32:31.382 --> 00:32:35.352 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> that means
 "stripper and eater of bark."</i>

00:32:35.352 --> 00:32:37.388
♪ ♪

00:32:37.388 --> 00:32:43.227
♪ ♪

00:32:43.227 --> 00:32:45.863
<i> Vacation time is over.</i>

00:32:45.863 --> 00:32:47.431
<i> But there's still
 a couple of months</i>

00:32:47.431 --> 00:32:50.000
<i> before the creatures
 of the Arctic Circle</i>

00:32:50.000 --> 00:32:53.437
<i> are plunged into darkness.</i>

00:32:53.437 --> 00:32:57.041 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> They must work with
 what light they have left.</i>

00:32:57.041 --> 00:33:02.079 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> Every week, the days grow
 shorter by more than an hour.</i>

00:33:02.079 --> 00:33:08.552
♪ ♪

00:33:08.552 --> 00:33:11.856 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> White-tailed eagles were
 nearly pushed to extinction</i>

00:33:11.856 --> 00:33:13.758
<i> throughout the region.</i>

00:33:13.758 --> 00:33:17.995 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> But they've been able to keep
 a toehold along the coast.</i>

00:33:17.995 --> 00:33:23.667
♪ ♪

00:33:23.667 --> 00:33:25.703
<i> Norway has the highest density</i>

00:33:25.703 --> 00:33:29.139 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> of white-tailed eagles
 in Europe.</i>

00:33:29.139 --> 00:33:34.712
♪ ♪

00:33:34.712 --> 00:33:38.882 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> With good light,
 it's a fish-finding frenzy.</i>

00:33:38.882 --> 00:33:43.921
♪ ♪

00:33:43.921 --> 00:33:49.560
♪ ♪

00:33:49.560 --> 00:33:53.631
<i> Not all the fish here
 are such hard work.</i>

00:33:53.631 --> 00:33:55.666
♪ ♪

00:33:55.666 --> 00:34:00.805
♪ ♪

00:34:00.805 --> 00:34:05.176 align:start position:0%
<i> Eco-safari guides like
 Trygve Steen serve up the sushi</i>

00:34:05.176 --> 00:34:08.980 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> as fishermen have done here
 for hundreds of years.</i>

00:34:10.581 --> 00:34:12.249
<i> Visitors can get a close look</i>

00:34:12.249 --> 00:34:16.387
<i> at some of this area's
 300 nesting couples.</i>

00:34:16.387 --> 00:34:20.257
♪ ♪

00:34:20.257 --> 00:34:22.726 align:start size:97% position:3%
TRYGVE STEEN:
It's a majestic bird, indeed.

00:34:22.726 --> 00:34:24.862
Also an unpredictable bird.

00:34:24.862 --> 00:34:27.264
They are able to make
their own choices.

00:34:27.264 --> 00:34:32.303
♪ ♪

00:34:32.303 --> 00:34:36.173
♪ ♪

00:34:36.173 --> 00:34:38.342
(squawking)

00:34:38.342 --> 00:34:44.782
♪ ♪

00:34:44.782 --> 00:34:51.755
♪ ♪

00:34:51.755 --> 00:34:58.562
♪ ♪

00:34:58.562 --> 00:35:00.264 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> NARRATOR: Some of
 the white-tailed eagles</i>

00:35:00.264 --> 00:35:03.534
<i> from Lofoten have been
 captured and relocated</i>

00:35:03.534 --> 00:35:05.369
<i> to bolster populations</i>

00:35:05.369 --> 00:35:08.339 align:start size:81% position:19%
<i> elsewhere in Europe
 and the U.K.</i>

00:35:11.275 --> 00:35:13.043
<i> That's helped spread the word</i>

00:35:13.043 --> 00:35:16.780 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> and made this area
 a hot spot for bird-watchers.</i>

00:35:16.780 --> 00:35:23.387
♪ ♪

00:35:23.387 --> 00:35:28.793
♪ ♪

00:35:35.098 --> 00:35:39.736 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> The fish they snatch
 are herring.</i>

00:35:39.736 --> 00:35:42.740
<i> And Norway is famous for
 the millions of herring</i>

00:35:42.740 --> 00:35:45.910 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> that migrate through here
 every autumn.</i>

00:35:48.445 --> 00:35:52.716
<i> Fishing trawlers wait for them.</i>

00:35:52.716 --> 00:35:56.554
<i> So, too, age-old predators.</i>

00:35:58.289 --> 00:36:02.459
<i> The birds signal
 the catch is good.</i>

00:36:02.459 --> 00:36:07.230
<i> Soon enough, around the boat...</i>

00:36:07.230 --> 00:36:09.000
<i> giants.</i>

00:36:13.003 --> 00:36:16.641 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> Humpback whales come in
 to take their share.</i>

00:36:18.642 --> 00:36:22.780
<i> The fishermen tighten their
 nets to haul in their catch.</i>

00:36:25.116 --> 00:36:28.519 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> But inevitably
 some herring escape.</i>

00:36:28.519 --> 00:36:32.156 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> And the humpbacks swoop by
 to collect them.</i>

00:36:34.692 --> 00:36:38.496 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> Each of these giants can be
 more than 50 feet long</i>

00:36:38.496 --> 00:36:41.632
<i> and weigh up to 40 tons.</i>

00:36:45.135 --> 00:36:49.040 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> And they can eat
 a ton of food a day.</i>

00:36:51.041 --> 00:36:56.413
<i> Sometimes large amounts of the
 tiniest creatures, like krill.</i>

00:36:56.413 --> 00:37:01.585 align:start position:0%
<i> Other times, like here,
 scraps of herring do just fine.</i>

00:37:01.585 --> 00:37:05.556
♪ ♪

00:37:05.556 --> 00:37:11.628 align:start position:0%
<i> This feast is too rich for just
 one whale species to enjoy.</i>

00:37:11.628 --> 00:37:16.133
♪ ♪

00:37:16.133 --> 00:37:21.104
<i> The fjords of Norway plunge
 steep and deep into the sea.</i>

00:37:21.104 --> 00:37:25.810 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> So orca don't mind
 coming in close to land.</i>

00:37:25.810 --> 00:37:29.446 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> They stand out in a crowd
 with pectoral fins</i>

00:37:29.446 --> 00:37:32.450 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> that can be more than
 six feet high.</i>

00:37:34.351 --> 00:37:39.022 align:start position:0%
<i> These social creatures can
 travel in pods up to 30 strong.</i>

00:37:39.022 --> 00:37:45.729
♪ ♪

00:37:45.729 --> 00:37:52.436
♪ ♪

00:37:52.436 --> 00:37:56.140
<i> They join their humpback
 cousins for the feast.</i>

00:37:59.810 --> 00:38:01.412
<i> And if two mammal species</i>

00:38:01.412 --> 00:38:05.883 align:start position:0%
<i> swimming for their supper here
 isn't enough, don't worry.</i>

00:38:05.883 --> 00:38:08.619
<i> Here comes a third.</i>

00:38:16.526 --> 00:38:18.495 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> NARRATOR: While the rest
 of Scandinavia</i>

00:38:18.495 --> 00:38:23.067
<i> may be hunkered down preparing
 for winter and its darkness,</i>

00:38:23.067 --> 00:38:27.838
<i> the sea is where you can
 still find a hearty meal.</i>

00:38:30.107 --> 00:38:33.277 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> The Gulf Stream keeps
 the water around the fjords</i>

00:38:33.277 --> 00:38:35.646
<i> virtually ice-free.</i>

00:38:35.646 --> 00:38:40.451 align:start position:0%
<i> That's what brings in the fish
 and the divers.</i>

00:38:40.451 --> 00:38:43.220 align:start size:97% position:3%
BORJAN DYRENES: We're really,
really, really lucky

00:38:43.220 --> 00:38:45.789 align:start size:84% position:16%
to live in this place
on the earth.

00:38:45.789 --> 00:38:49.293
We have the Gulf current
hitting the shore here.

00:38:49.293 --> 00:38:54.464 align:start position:0%
The water is constantly between
15 and 6 degrees Celsius,

00:38:54.464 --> 00:39:00.036
and the nutrition of the water
here, it has a really rich life,

00:39:00.036 --> 00:39:02.540
both for mammals and for fish.

00:39:05.576 --> 00:39:08.345 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> NARRATOR: Borjan Dyrenes
 and his dive buddies</i>

00:39:08.345 --> 00:39:12.015
<i> use modern gear and can stay
 in the water for a few hours</i>

00:39:12.015 --> 00:39:14.285
<i> without getting too cold.</i>

00:39:16.019 --> 00:39:19.289 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> They seek out flatfish,
 pollack, haddock,</i>

00:39:19.289 --> 00:39:21.692
<i> and especially cod.</i>

00:39:23.427 --> 00:39:27.231 align:start position:0%
BORJAN: We're planning each dive
before we go into the water.

00:39:27.231 --> 00:39:30.768 align:start size:88% position:13%
What kind of fish
do we want to look for?

00:39:34.538 --> 00:39:37.741
Everything, like, in the sea
comes really close to you,

00:39:37.741 --> 00:39:39.776 align:start size:81% position:19%
especially when
you're a freediver.

00:39:39.776 --> 00:39:46.350
♪ ♪

00:39:46.350 --> 00:39:49.219 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> NARRATOR: Freedivers
 don't use tanks,</i>

00:39:49.219 --> 00:39:54.624 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> but can hold their breath for
 several minutes at a time.</i>

00:39:54.624 --> 00:39:57.627 align:start size:88% position:13%
BORJAN: You have to keep
all your senses open,

00:39:57.627 --> 00:40:01.399
because the fish senses you,
you know, and your excitement.

00:40:05.035 --> 00:40:07.237 align:start size:78% position:22%
And it triggers
all your instincts

00:40:07.237 --> 00:40:09.272
when you go in the water.

00:40:09.272 --> 00:40:14.778
♪ ♪

00:40:14.778 --> 00:40:17.247 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> NARRATOR: In the 1960s,
 there was a push</i>

00:40:17.247 --> 00:40:20.884
<i> to make spearfishing
 an Olympic sport.</i>

00:40:20.884 --> 00:40:26.624
<i> That failed, but the fish here
 continue to attract divers.</i>

00:40:29.393 --> 00:40:30.928 align:start size:91% position:9%
BORJAN: You can catch fish
all year here,

00:40:30.928 --> 00:40:33.130
and there's probably
plenty enough fish

00:40:33.130 --> 00:40:34.365
to feed my whole family

00:40:34.365 --> 00:40:37.200
and the neighbors and
their neighbors again.

00:40:37.200 --> 00:40:38.868
So I shoot a lot of fish here

00:40:38.868 --> 00:40:41.271 align:start size:81% position:19%
and I give them away
to my neighbors,

00:40:41.271 --> 00:40:43.207
I give them away to my family.

00:40:43.207 --> 00:40:46.576 align:start size:88% position:13%
Yeah. It's really nice,
really nice.

00:40:46.576 --> 00:40:48.612
♪ ♪

00:40:48.612 --> 00:40:55.252
♪ ♪

00:40:55.252 --> 00:40:58.255
<i> NARRATOR: The biggest fish
 come to the lower regions</i>

00:40:58.255 --> 00:41:02.159
<i> of the Arctic Circle in winter.</i>

00:41:02.159 --> 00:41:04.861 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> But one of
 the strangest-looking species</i>

00:41:04.861 --> 00:41:10.568 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> never leaves,
 no matter how dark it gets.</i>

00:41:12.202 --> 00:41:17.140
♪ ♪

00:41:17.140 --> 00:41:21.478 align:start position:0%
<i> The vibrant Atlantic wolffish,
 or sea wolf,</i>

00:41:21.478 --> 00:41:24.381
<i> resembles an eel,</i>

00:41:24.381 --> 00:41:29.186 align:start size:78% position:22%
<i> with one long fin
 down its back.</i>

00:41:29.186 --> 00:41:33.490
<i> But its real magic
 is on the inside.</i>

00:41:33.490 --> 00:41:38.695
<i> The wolffish produces a kind
 of antifreeze for its blood</i>

00:41:38.695 --> 00:41:42.466 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> so it can live in the icy
 Arctic all year round,</i>

00:41:42.466 --> 00:41:45.736 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> even now in the depths
 of winter.</i>

00:41:47.804 --> 00:41:52.376 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> It dines on shellfish
 and starfish and the like,</i>

00:41:52.376 --> 00:41:57.347 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> which it can rip into
 with savage teeth.</i>

00:41:57.347 --> 00:41:59.116
<i> They are one of the few fish</i>

00:41:59.116 --> 00:42:03.053
<i> to have both parents
 care for their eggs.</i>

00:42:03.053 --> 00:42:08.125 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> They guard them for several
 months until they hatch.</i>

00:42:08.125 --> 00:42:12.662 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> Their home here in the
 Arctic Circle is also unique.</i>

00:42:12.662 --> 00:42:18.001 align:start position:0%
<i> It's the world's largest known
 cold water reef.</i>

00:42:18.001 --> 00:42:21.071 align:start position:0%
<i> With all this color, it's hard
 to believe it's been dark</i>

00:42:21.071 --> 00:42:24.007 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> up top for more than
 two months.</i>

00:42:24.007 --> 00:42:26.976 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> Except perhaps for
 the returning shimmer</i>

00:42:26.976 --> 00:42:29.146
<i> of the northern lights.</i>

00:42:29.146 --> 00:42:31.181
♪ ♪

00:42:31.181 --> 00:42:37.454
♪ ♪

00:42:37.454 --> 00:42:43.059 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> Then, once again,
 a glimmer of sunlight.</i>

00:42:43.059 --> 00:42:47.264
<i> And warmth starts
 to melt the snow.</i>

00:42:50.033 --> 00:42:52.936
<i> These extremes of
 the Arctic Circle</i>

00:42:52.936 --> 00:42:55.406
<i> force humans...</i>

00:42:58.108 --> 00:43:00.377
<i> and creatures...</i>

00:43:04.314 --> 00:43:07.350
<i> ...to toughen up.</i>

00:43:07.350 --> 00:43:10.254
<i> They must adapt
 to conditions...</i>

00:43:13.323 --> 00:43:17.694
<i> ...and embrace hardship.</i>

00:43:17.694 --> 00:43:21.064
<i> But once a year, like magic...</i>

00:43:21.064 --> 00:43:22.733
(creaking)

00:43:24.768 --> 00:43:29.206 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> The veil of darkness lifts
 throughout the region.</i>

00:43:29.206 --> 00:43:31.675
(creaking)

00:43:31.675 --> 00:43:34.578
<i> The ice melts away.</i>

00:43:34.578 --> 00:43:38.148
<i> And the Arctic has
 come full circle.</i>

00:43:38.148 --> 00:43:39.516
(creaking)

00:43:41.151 --> 00:43:45.555 line:0%
<i> Time again to decide how to
 spend your summer vacation.</i>

00:43:45.555 --> 00:43:50.661 line:0%
♪ ♪

00:43:50.661 --> 00:43:55.299 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
<i> Holidays in the Arctic
 are short.</i>

00:43:55.299 --> 00:43:59.569 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
<i> But that's why they
 may be more prized here</i>

00:43:59.569 --> 00:44:03.106 align:start size:81% position:19% line:0%
<i> than anywhere else
 on the planet.</i>

00:44:03.106 --> 00:44:09.313 line:0%
♪ ♪

00:44:09.313 --> 00:44:11.215 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
